# Testing Standards

## Framework and Structure
- Use JUnit as the testing framework
- Organize tests in the `src/test/kotlin` directory structure within each module
- Mirror the main source package structure in tests
- Domain tests should only test business logic without external dependencies
- App tests can include integration tests with external dependencies

## Test Naming Conventions
- Use backtick descriptive naming for test methods
- Example: `should process valid CFDI successfully`
- Example: `should throw ValidationException when CFDI is malformed`
- Write descriptive test names that explain the behavior being tested

## Test Organization
- Group related tests in the same test class
- Use `@Nested` inner classes for logical grouping when appropriate
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern

## Assertions and Mocking
- Use JUnit 5 assertions (`assertThat`, `assertEquals`, etc.)
- NEVER add comments or error messages in assertEquals and other assertions - only compare the properties themselves
- Do NOT pass the "display this in case of an error" parameter to assertions
- In unit tests always use mockk() instead of annotations like @Mockk and always have a @AfterEach with confirmVerified
- All verifies must be fully qualified, don't use any(), at most, use withArg if needed, or TestUtils.equalsWithTimeTolerance

## Test Data
- Keep test data close to the tests that use it
- Use meaningful test data that reflects real-world scenarios
- Avoid hardcoded values when possible - use constants or builders

## Module-Specific Testing
- **Domain Module Tests**: Test pure business logic, mock all external dependencies
- **App Module Tests**: Test implementations, can include integration tests with real dependencies
- Use dependency injection in tests following the same patterns as production code
